# What must NEVER reach a build context, and what would make the image depend on
# THIS machine. Both lists are short and both are load-bearing.
#
# 1. CREDENTIALS. `.env` is the only place secrets live (SPEC §3.1) and it is
#    excluded here as well as in .gitignore, so a `COPY . .` cannot bake one into
#    a layer even by accident. `docker compose` hands it to the CONTAINER at run
#    time (`env_file:`), which is a different thing from putting it in an image:
#    one is readable by anyone who pulls the image, the other is not.
#
# 2. THE HOST'S BUILD OUTPUT. `web/node_modules` is resolved for darwin/arm64 and
#    `web/.next` was built against it; copying either would make the image a
#    photograph of this laptop and would break on a reviewer's. The Dockerfiles
#    run `npm ci` and `next build` themselves, from the committed lockfile, which
#    is the only way "works from a clean checkout" can be true rather than hoped.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

**/node_modules
**/.next
web/next-env.d.ts
web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

.git
.github
.venv
venv
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.pytest_cache

# Nothing either service runs: the issue tracker, the agent scratch, the design
# mockups, the brief, and the screenshots the browser layer diffs on the HOST.
.beads
.dolt
.claude
.codex
.agents
.sageox
.bv
design
tests/e2e/__baselines__
*.pdf
